00:01:08.746 --> 00:01:12.838
<v HUGO> Hi Chantal. What did you think of the talk, then?

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:17.691
<v CHANTAL> Hi Hugo. I thought it was good once I'd moved seats.

00:01:17.692 --> 00:01:20.633
<v HUGO> Oh – were the people beside you chatting or something?

00:01:20.635 --> 00:01:30.991
<v CHANTAL> It wasn't that. I went early so that I'd get a seat and not have to stand, but then this guy sat right in front of me and he was so tall!

00:01:30.992 --> 00:01:33.591
<v HUGO> It's hard to see through people's heads, isn't it?

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:43.787
<v CHANTAL> Impossible! Anyway, to answer your question, I thought it was really interesting, especially what the speaker said about the job market.

00:01:43.788 --> 00:01:51.186
<v HUGO> Me too. I mean we know we're going into a really competitive field so it's obvious that we may struggle to get work.

00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:56.251
<v CHANTAL> That's right – and we know we can't all have that 'dream job'.

00:01:56.253 --> 00:02:04.705
<v HUGO> Yeah, but it looks like there's a whole range of ... areas of work that we hadn't even thought of – like fashion journalism, for instance.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:08.443
<v CHANTAL> Yeah – I wasn't expecting so many career options.

00:02:08.443 --> 00:02:12.669
<v HUGO> Mmm. Overall, she had quite a strong message, didn't she?

00:02:12.671 --> 00:02:20.553
<v CHANTAL> She did. She kept saying things like ‘I know you all think this, but…’ and then she'd tell us how it really is.

00:02:20.554 --> 00:02:24.633
<v HUGO> Perhaps she thinks students are a bit narrow-minded about the industry.

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:28.700
<v CHANTAL> It was a bit harsh, though! We know it's a tough industry.

00:02:28.702 --> 00:02:33.112
<v HUGO> Yeah – and we're only first years, after all. We've got a lot to learn.

00:02:33.114 --> 00:02:39.699
<v CHANTAL> Exactly. Do you think our secondary-school education should have been more career-focused?

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:49.381
<v HUGO> Well, we had numerous talks on careers, which was good, but none of them were very inspiring. They could have asked more people like today's speaker to talk to us.

00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:59.350
<v CHANTAL> I agree. We were told about lots of different careers – just when we needed to be, but not by the experts who really know stuff.

00:02:59.351 --> 00:03:04.519
<v HUGO> So did today's talk influence your thoughts on what career you'd like to take up in the future?

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.714
<v CHANTAL> Well, I promised myself that I'd go through this course and keep an open mind till the end.

00:03:09.716 --> 00:03:15.679
<v HUGO> But I think it's better to pick an area of the industry now and then aim to get better and better at it.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.746
<v CHANTAL> Well, I think we'll just have to differ on that issue!

00:03:19.748 --> 00:03:26.103
<v HUGO> One thing's for certain, though. From what she said, we'll be unpaid assistants in the industry for quite a long time.

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:26.894
<v CHANTAL> Mmm.

00:03:26.896 --> 00:03:29.404
<v HUGO> I'm prepared for that, aren't you?

00:03:29.406 --> 00:03:32.799
<v CHANTAL> Actually, I'm not going to accept that view.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.119
<v HUGO> Really? But she knows it's the case – and everyone else says the same.

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:40.566
<v CHANTAL> That doesn't mean it has to be true for me.

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:44.061
<v HUGO> OK. Well – I hope you're right!

00:04:19.713 --> 00:04:24.396
<v CHANTAL> I thought the speaker's account of her first job was fascinating.

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:34.631
<v HUGO> Yeah – she admitted she was lucky to get work being a personal dresser for a musician. She didn't even apply for the job and there she was getting paid to choose all his clothes.

00:04:34.632 --> 00:04:43.243
<v CHANTAL> It must have felt amazing – though she said all she was looking for back then was experience, not financial reward.

00:04:43.245 --> 00:04:49.177
<v HUGO> Mmm. And then he was so mean, telling her she was more interested in her own appearance than his!

00:04:49.179 --> 00:05:01.976
<v CHANTAL> But – she did realise he was right about that, which really made me think. I'm always considering my own clothes but now I can see you should be focusing on your client!

00:05:01.977 --> 00:05:04.571
<v HUGO> She obviously regretted losing the job.

00:05:04.573 --> 00:05:10.692
<v CHANTAL> Well, as she said, she should have hidden her negative feelings about him, but she didn't.

00:05:10.694 --> 00:05:17.812
<v HUGO> It was really brave the way she picked herself up and took that job in retail. Fancy working in a shop after that!

00:05:17.814 --> 00:05:30.497
<v CHANTAL> Yeah – well, she recommended we all do it at some point. I guess as a designer you'd get to find out some useful information, like how big or small the average shopper is.

00:05:30.499 --> 00:05:42.106
<v HUGO> I think that's an issue for manufacturers, not designers. However, it would be useful to know if there's a gap in the market – you know, an item that no one's stocking but that consumers are looking for.

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:48.862
<v CHANTAL> Yeah, people don't give up searching. They also take things back to the store if they aren't right.

00:05:48.864 --> 00:05:57.354
<v HUGO> Yeah. Imagine you worked in an expensive shop and you found out the garments sold there were being returned because they... fell apart in the wash!

00:05:57.355 --> 00:06:00.361
<v CHANTAL> Yeah, it would be good to know that kind of thing.

00:06:00.362 --> 00:06:01.138
<v HUGO> Yeah.